P0 configurationPros and Cons
Main advantage, this is the most cost to effectiveness ratio configuration:
- Limited impact of the 48V system on the existing vehicle architecture;
- Vehicle and transmission architecture can be kept the same as for a conventional vehicle;
- The 48V electric machine can be easily integrated into the already existing ICE accessories belt drive
replacing the 12V alternator (generator);
- In one word: Cheap
Disadvantages:
- Traditional engine and electric machine mechanically linked through the accessory belt, so engine friction
will be a parasitic loss for the electric machine when it gives boost torque and when it’s recuperating
electrical energy;
- Belt durability needs to be increased to sustain higher torque and more engine off/on cycles;
- Belt slip can limit the amount of transmitted torque, so current BSG applications still use the 12V starter
for cold engine start that demands high electric machine torque due to high engine friction.

Main advantage, the removal of the belt drive:
- Slight increase in efficiency;
- Higher electric machine torque in terms of amplitude and response time (compared to P0);
- Electric creeping possible;
Disadvantages:
- Higher cost;
- Higher impact on existing vehicle architecture (crankshaft has to be changed, integration require re-
design of the engine allocation);
- Electric machine is still on ICE side (no mechanical disconnection possible).
P1 configurationPros and Cons

Main advantage, electric machine mounted on driveline side (EM can be detached from ICE):
- Increased energy recuperation potential;
- Very high increase in torque boosting efficiency (moving from P2 to P4, less part to move);
- Electric creep or even electric drive;
- Higher range of coasting strategies;
- Electric 4WD (P4 only)
Disadvantages:
- High impact on existing vehicle architecture;
- Higher overall cost of the electrical components;
- Air cooling not possible for the electric machine;
- S&S inhibited.
